From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: actually disable fastmem when its handler will not install

    fatal: signal 11 - SIGSEGV (bad address), si_code 1, fault address 0
    mc_trapno 0xc (page fault), mc_addr 0, mc_rip 0x249303801, mc_rflags 0x10206

⚠ mc_rip IS NOT IN ANY MODULE. 0x249303801 is heap - the recompiler's OWN GENERATED CODE - while
the core loads at 0x800870000. So the fault is not in swanstation's C++; it is in code swanstation
wrote, dereferencing a base pointer of zero.

That pointer is Bus::m_fastmem_base. InitializeFastmem returns early when the page-fault handler
cannot be installed - which it cannot here, the log says so - and the early return SKIPS the two
calls immediately below it:

    Bus::UpdateFastmemViews(mode);
    CPU::UpdateFastmemBase();

⚠ AND BOTH CALL SITES DISCARD THE FAILURE, which is what turns a missing feature into a crash:

    if (g_settings.IsUsingFastmem() && !InitializeFastmem()) { }

An empty block. So the setting stays on, m_fastmem_base stays nullptr, and the recompiler keeps
emitting loads and stores through it. This makes the failure path do what the success path does:
put the mode back to Disabled and tell the CPU, so the generated code stops addressing through a
pointer that was never set.

The handler itself is a separate question - this kernel delivers signals with FreeBSD's original
(sig, code, scp) signature unless SA_SIGINFO carries FreeBSD's value, which orbis-compat now
corrects; whether swanstation's own installer can be made to work here is untested.

diff --git a/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp b/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp
--- a/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp
+++ b/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp
@@ -1003,6 +1003,25 @@
                                                 s_code_buffer.GetTotalSize(), handler))
   {
     Log_ErrorPrintf("Failed to install page fault handler");
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+    // ⚠ TURNING FASTMEM OFF FOR REAL, BECAUSE FAILING TO INSTALL THE HANDLER DOES NOT DO IT.
+    // The two calls below this block - UpdateFastmemViews and UpdateFastmemBase - are skipped by
+    // this early return, and BOTH callers ignore the result:
+    //
+    //     if (g_settings.IsUsingFastmem() && !InitializeFastmem()) { }
+    //
+    // So m_fastmem_base stays nullptr while the recompiler goes on emitting code that addresses
+    // guest memory THROUGH it. Measured on hardware: the fault was at address 0, with mc_rip in
+    // the recompiler's own generated code rather than in any module -
+    //
+    //     fatal: signal 11 - SIGSEGV, si_code 1 (SEGV_MAPERR), fault address 0
+    //     mc_trapno 0xc, mc_addr 0, mc_rip 0x249303801
+    //
+    // Disabling it here is the difference between "fastmem is unavailable" and "fastmem is
+    // unavailable and the generated code does not know".
+    Bus::UpdateFastmemViews(CPUFastmemMode::Disabled);
+    CPU::UpdateFastmemBase();
+#endif
     return false;
   }
 
